<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cap-Haïtien</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cap-Français, Cap-Henri</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cap-Haïtien is a port on the north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>. Founded by the French as Cap-Français, it was the wealthy capital of the colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a> until 1770 and was burned in 1793 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-revolution">Haitian Revolution</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-christophe">Henri Christophe</a> was crowned there as King Henri I in 1811, when it was renamed Cap-Henri; his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/citadelle-laferriere">Citadelle Laferrière</a> and Sans-Souci palace stand nearby.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cap-haitien.jpg" alt="Cap-Haïtien" width="320" height="174" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rémi Kaupp · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cap-Haitien_seen_from_Morne_Jean.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>An earthquake in May 1842 destroyed <b>Cap-Haïtien</b> and other northern towns, and the following year the Reform movement led by Charles Rivière-Hérard rose in the south; Boyer resigned and left for exile in March 1843.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-haiti-1806-1849">Republic of Haiti (1806-1849) · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Cap-Français</b>, later Cap-Haïtien, was established shortly before 1670, when a tobacco crisis emptied many places and freebooting revived in raids such as Vera Cruz in 1683 and Campêche in 1686.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>At the top of colonial society stood the grands blancs, the “big whites”: the owners of the great sugar estates, the leading merchants of <b>Cap-Français</b> and Port-au-Prince, and the senior officials and officers.</blockquote><a href="/societies/saint-domingue">Social Hierarchy of Saint-Domingue</a></li><li><blockquote>He was crowned Henri I at <b>Cap-Haïtien</b>, renamed Cap-Henri, in June 1811, while Alexandre Pétion's Republic of Haiti continued to rule the south and west.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-haiti">Kingdom of Haiti</a></li><li><blockquote>In February 1807 he set up his own State of Haiti, with its capital at <b>Cap-Haïtien</b> and himself as president for life.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-haiti">State of Haiti</a></li></ul></section>
